<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehmed II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1432">1432</a>–<a href="/years/1481">1481</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehmed II was an Ottoman sultan who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Known as the Conqueror, he made the city the Ottoman capital and expanded the empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. His reign established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> as a major world power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii.jpg" alt="Mehmed II" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gentile Bellini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bellini,_Gentile_-_Sultan_Mehmet_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His ambitions then turned west, where the Ottoman sultan <b>Mehmed II</b> had annexed Trebizond in 1461 despite Aq Qoyunlu protests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Constantine XI's death fighting on the walls when <b>Mehmed II</b> took Constantinople on 29 May 1453 — the last Roman emperor dying in defence of the last Roman city — was one of history's most symbolically complete endings, closing eleven centuries of continuous institutional existence with a final…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1461 the Ottoman sultan <b>Mehmed II</b> brought a powerful army across the mountains while his fleet blockaded Trebizond by sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The conquest of Constantinople on 29 May 1453 by <b>Mehmed II</b>, achieved after a siege of some fifty-three days in which great bronze cannon cast by the founder Urban breached the Theodosian walls that had resisted assault for over a thousand years, was the empire’s defining transformation: it…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan <b>Mehmed II</b> himself invaded in 1476 and defeated Stephen at Valea Albă (Războieni), but failed to take Suceava or Neamț and withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1453 Giovanni Giustiniani Longo led the defence of the land walls of Constantinople against <b>Mehmed II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Gebze, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.67,26.57&amp;year=1463" aria-label="Edirne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mehmed II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.67,26.57&amp;year=1463">What was here in 1463? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The spider has wove his web in the Imperial palace; and the owl hath sung her watch-song on the towers of Afrasiab.&#8221;</p><footer>In the palace of the emperors, Constantinople, 1453 (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 68) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; a Persian couplet he recited, as Gibbon tells it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/736/pg736-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
